    "created_at": "2020-06-15T14:30:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-06-15T14:30:57Z",
    "body": "The default branch name is coded into git to be `master`. You can check this yourself:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit init --quiet && cat .git/HEAD\r\n# ref: refs/heads/master\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat is why that first commit made automatically creates a branch called master, it is already prepared. This is also why `git status` will tell you you are “On branch master” even when `git branch` is empty.\r\n\r\nAlthough something in Starship seems to be handling git wrong anyway. Because changing the file to point at a different name has no effect:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit init --quiet\r\necho \"ref: refs/heads/default\" > .git/HEAD\r\ngit status\r\n# On branch default\r\n# \r\n# No commits yet\r\n# \r\n# nothing to commit (create/copy files and use \"git add\" to track)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis still renders `master` in my Starship prompt, even though I expect `default`.",
